1
00:00:02,060 --> 00:00:05,649
[nm]: The suttas often talk about sense objects.

2
00:00:07,990 --> 00:00:14,283
[nm]: So, what is... how would you explain to, say, somebody that wants to practice...

3
00:00:17,006 --> 00:00:17,947
[nm]: What is a sense object?

4
00:00:17,960 --> 00:00:19,160
[nm]: What are sense objects?

5
00:00:19,243 --> 00:00:20,738
[nm]: How do you find them in your experience?

6
00:00:20,750 --> 00:00:22,043
[nm]: How do you define them rightly?

7
00:00:27,073 --> 00:00:28,787
[ng]: Well, first of all, you have to be...

8
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:48,298
[ng]: restrained in your actions, because through your actions, you put yourself in a position where you're already immersed in sense objects, and therefore you're not able to even see them as arisen things that you could get immersed in.

9
00:00:49,763 --> 00:00:55,916
[ng]: So, in order to be able to see what a sense object is, you basically have to

10
00:00:57,100 --> 00:01:04,873
[ng]: look at what are the ways in which, regardless of what views you have about the sense objects, you would already be involved with them.

11
00:01:05,535 --> 00:01:08,587
[ng]: And so that's sensuality, not keeping the precepts.

12
00:01:09,210 --> 00:01:19,676
[ng]: These kinds of actions, like, even though you might then philosophize about what sense objects are, you're already completely tunnel-visioned into them, simply by living in that way.

13
00:01:20,886 --> 00:01:35,643
[ng]: So then, if that's established, you're mostly withdrawn from those actions, then sense objects would kind of be the... it's... you... they're not things that you can...

14
00:01:37,510 --> 00:01:47,340
[ng]: observe or get focused on, in the same way as, you know, the... or the wrong way that people would go about it, is trying to focus on their sensations, usually.

15
00:01:48,120 --> 00:01:56,169
[ng]: But... and this idea that it's zoning in on them and zooming in on them.

16
00:01:56,630 --> 00:02:01,640
[ng]: But really, you have to see, sense objects includes the mind as well, mental objects.

17
00:02:02,290 --> 00:02:07,230
[ng]: Those are the ones that most people are often not aware of and not clear about.

18
00:02:07,738 --> 00:02:27,181
[ng]: That these are options of... you can almost call them like options of exploration, options of going down and exploring the particulars, as the suttas describe.

19
00:02:27,744 --> 00:02:30,433
[ng]: And the task is not to then

20
00:02:31,200 --> 00:02:38,723
[ng]: find a particular definition of what sense objects are, but learn how to not get absorbed in those particulars as such.

21
00:02:38,729 --> 00:02:43,513
[ng]: See them as options that you could get absorbed in, but you choose not to.

22
00:02:44,375 --> 00:02:50,809
[nm]: So, but, I mean, one could argue that sight, sound, smell, taste, touch is something you can zoom in on.

23
00:02:51,892 --> 00:02:53,138
[ng]: Yeah, you can, but that's the thing.

24
00:02:53,418 --> 00:02:56,166
[ng]: If you do, you lose perspective of...

25
00:02:58,020 --> 00:03:04,424
[ng]: not. Because the thing is, you don't want to have perspective of the sense-objects, you want to have perspective over the dimension of the senses.

26
00:03:05,409 --> 00:03:05,640
[nm]: Right.

27
00:03:05,640 --> 00:03:08,255
[nm]: But the question is: are those the sense-objects?

28
00:03:09,424 --> 00:03:09,858
[nm]: What do you mean?

29
00:03:09,858 --> 00:03:09,861
[nm]: Sight, sound, smell, taste, touches.
[nm]: What do you mean?

30
00:03:09,861 --> 00:03:11,123
[nm]: Sight, sound, smell, taste, touches.

31
00:03:11,890 --> 00:03:20,873
[ng]: Yeah, but... like, those are sense-objects, but that's not where the problem... that's not where your development should be.

32
00:03:20,880 --> 00:03:22,763
[ng]: It shouldn't be about examining them.

33
00:03:23,360 --> 00:03:24,529
[ng]: Or zooming on them further.

34
00:03:24,910 --> 00:03:30,852
[ng]: You want to see the domain of the eye, the domain of the ear, the domain of the...

35
00:03:30,990 --> 00:03:31,498
[nm]: What is that?

36
00:03:31,498 --> 00:03:31,507
[nm]: That's too abstract.
[nm]: What is that?

37
00:03:31,507 --> 00:03:32,861
[nm]: That's too abstract.

38
00:03:33,470 --> 00:03:38,240
[nm]: That's too abstract for, say, anybody that's not familiar with philosophy.

39
00:03:38,249 --> 00:03:38,796
[nm]: It's like...

40
00:03:38,796 --> 00:03:38,800
[nm]: So, I want to practice.
[nm]: It's like...

41
00:03:38,800 --> 00:03:41,243
[nm]: So, I want to practice.

42
00:03:41,646 --> 00:03:43,283
[nm]: The suttas talk about sense objects.

43
00:03:45,280 --> 00:03:52,624
[nm]: You said I shouldn't be zooming in on them, shouldn't be... how do you say... lose perspective, and so on.

44
00:03:54,526 --> 00:03:56,076
[nm]: And I should be seeing the domain instead.

45
00:03:56,090 --> 00:03:57,313
[nm]: What is that? What is the...

46
00:03:57,316 --> 00:03:58,270
[nm]: How do I make sense of that?

47
00:03:58,960 --> 00:04:01,363
[ng]: Well, the domain is... like...

48
00:04:01,580 --> 00:04:02,073
[ng]: Practically.

49
00:04:02,080 --> 00:04:02,855
[ng]: Give me an example.

50
00:04:02,850 --> 00:04:15,178
[ng]: Where you find the pressure to go, to examine further, to look further, to smell further, such as sensuality in general, pleasant sense objects.

51
00:04:15,930 --> 00:04:22,532
[ng]: Things that you're not just... You shouldn't just examine random neutral sense object in the world.

52
00:04:23,286 --> 00:04:26,843
[ng]: You will understand this principle if you go where the pressure is.

53
00:04:27,360 --> 00:04:32,787
[ng]: Things that are emotionally charged in one way or another, that you can't just be indifferent towards.

54
00:04:33,301 --> 00:04:33,984
[nm]: And is the...

55
00:04:35,141 --> 00:04:39,289
[nm]: Right, so you're saying that one should look at the...

56
00:04:41,200 --> 00:04:50,713
[nm]: pressure that one feels towards sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, rather than examining sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches.

57
00:04:50,713 --> 00:04:53,273
[ng]: Is that correctly understood?

58
00:04:53,344 --> 00:04:53,504
[ng]: Yeah.

59
00:04:53,578 --> 00:05:04,970
[ng]: So, find the pressure first, and then, through that, you will get to see that principle of descending onto the particulars that the suttas describe.

60
00:05:04,990 --> 00:05:06,415
[nm]: But what if there is no pressure?

61
00:05:07,530 --> 00:05:08,760
[nm]: What if I can't see any pressure?

62
00:05:10,132 --> 00:05:20,006
[ng]: Well, if that's truly the case, then either you're an arahant or there's something that you're overlooking, something that you're still...

63
00:05:21,141 --> 00:05:22,960
[ng]: You see, you have to... you cannot be neutral.

64
00:05:23,750 --> 00:05:29,480
[ng]: You cannot be in like a neutral, completely uninvolved situation.

65
00:05:29,935 --> 00:05:34,690
[ng]: You have to be over-attending something.

66
00:05:36,126 --> 00:05:46,855
[ng]: Unless your mind is fully developed, you will be over-attending or not seeing, not having perspective in regard to some sense object, even if it's neutral, ambiguous, unclear.

67
00:05:47,249 --> 00:05:49,500
[nm]: In regard to sense objects, not having perspective?

68
00:05:49,740 --> 00:05:50,009
[ng]: Yeah, so...

69
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:50,420
[nm]: What do you mean?

70
00:05:50,500 --> 00:05:51,341
[ng]: Because the mental...

71
00:05:51,756 --> 00:06:01,476
[nm]: So, if I feel no pressure, I mean, like, somebody might legitimately say, well, I'm not pressured to look at anything, so what do I do then?

72
00:06:02,360 --> 00:06:06,412
[ng]: Well, but are you... is there something that you are... Like, I feel neutrally.

73
00:06:07,723 --> 00:06:08,732
[nm]: Like, I feel neutrally.

74
00:06:08,880 --> 00:06:11,723
[nm]: There's no pressure, there's no craving, there's no lust, there's no aversion.

75
00:06:11,738 --> 00:06:12,593
[nm]: I feel neutrally.

76
00:06:12,836 --> 00:06:13,940
[nm]: What... Where's the pressure?

77
00:06:14,083 --> 00:06:14,710
[nm]: What do I look for?

78
00:06:15,129 --> 00:06:15,970
[ng]: Well, but the thing is...

79
00:06:16,107 --> 00:06:17,624
[nm]: According to you, pressure is always there.

80
00:06:17,876 --> 00:06:18,030
[nm]: Yeah.

81
00:06:18,231 --> 00:06:18,824
[nm]: That's what you said.

82
00:06:18,824 --> 00:06:18,832
[nm]: So, where is it?
[nm]: That's what you said.

83
00:06:18,832 --> 00:06:19,590
[nm]: So, where is it?

84
00:06:19,756 --> 00:06:25,170
[ng]: Well, it's... pressure is always there, but it's not going to be always in a way that you expect to find it.

85
00:06:25,929 --> 00:06:26,612
[ng]: So, how do I find it?

86
00:06:27,184 --> 00:06:30,006
[ng]: You can, in that state of...

87
00:06:30,584 --> 00:06:55,584
[ng]: let's say, supposedly, not being pressured, you might not be pressured towards the specific things that you were pressured before, but can you truly say that if you just kind of take your hands off the situation, so to speak, that the mind won't be drifting into something on its own, such as boredom, or fantasizing things about the past, or

88
00:06:56,155 --> 00:07:06,518
[ng]: planning about the future, or even just the state of dullness, which kind of carries its own momentum, you didn't ask for it, it just comes like that on its own.

89
00:07:07,206 --> 00:07:25,981
[ng]: So then, if that's still there, that means... well, that is another experience, another object, I guess you could say, which you would also have to maintain perspective in regard to, that... those other directions that you would be falling down into.

90
00:07:27,203 --> 00:07:32,732
[ng]: When there's no pressure, you don't want to think as if, oh, there's nothing here.

91
00:07:32,800 --> 00:07:37,249
[nm]: So, in other words, just wait a bit and see, because the pressure will come up.

92
00:07:37,260 --> 00:07:37,384
[ng]: Yeah.

93
00:07:38,530 --> 00:07:46,526
[nm]: Yeah, or, if seemingly there is no pressure, it's not... well, it's either because, as you said, you're already in arahant, or it's because you're simply not discerning it at the time.

94
00:07:46,587 --> 00:07:46,775
[ng]: Yeah.

95
00:07:46,975 --> 00:07:53,350
[ng]: So, it will be clear that in that state, even when you thought there wasn't pressure, there was some.

96
00:07:55,827 --> 00:07:58,258
[ng]: Like a subtler pressure than what you're used to.

97
00:07:58,340 --> 00:08:07,264
[ng]: That doesn't... might not fit the definition of, like, the core sensuality that you were expecting, but clearly, the mind would be kind of tugging by itself.

98
00:08:07,260 --> 00:08:07,907
[nm]: It's not neutral.

99
00:08:07,916 --> 00:08:08,295
[ng]: Exactly.

100
00:08:09,676 --> 00:08:24,818
[nm]: So... so then, would you say that... so sense object... sense object is something you can attend to, focus on, concentrate, look at directly, but as... as you said, that's not the point.

101
00:08:26,010 --> 00:08:28,876
[nm]: The point is to look at the pressure that's making you do it.

102
00:08:29,150 --> 00:08:35,006
[ng]: Yeah, because in the suttas it's not just understanding the sense objects, it's understanding the sense bases.

103
00:08:36,283 --> 00:08:41,120
[ng]: And the sense bases are completely obscured to the extent that you descend into them.

104
00:08:41,330 --> 00:08:50,886
[nm]: But if I just, you know, study the sights, wouldn't that reveal me the nature of vision?

105
00:08:51,830 --> 00:08:52,787
[nm]: The nature of the eye...

106
00:08:53,250 --> 00:08:57,433
[ng]: Well, no, because it's the eye that you would be using to study the sights.

107
00:08:57,480 --> 00:09:02,313
[ng]: So, it's like a camera recording itself, kind of thing.

108
00:09:02,360 --> 00:09:05,603
[ng]: You can't possibly arrive at it through

109
00:09:06,701 --> 00:09:09,150
[ng]: examining the objects that come on account of it.

110
00:09:09,796 --> 00:09:21,587
[nm]: But what if I really, really study the sights, like, to the to the kind of microscopic degree, in between the moments, like, like, like, really, to the greatest detail?

111
00:09:21,780 --> 00:09:22,258
[ng]: But that's the thing...

112
00:09:22,252 --> 00:09:23,030
[nm]: Wouldn't that show me?

113
00:09:23,040 --> 00:09:31,790
[ng]: The eye, as the as the suttas describe, the eye is like, or all of the six senses, they're like these animals that are pulling you in separate directions.

114
00:09:31,790 --> 00:09:31,800
[ng]: So, it's not this kind of technical sort of mechanism that you discover.
[ng]: The eye, as the as the suttas describe, the eye is like, or all of the six senses, they're like these animals that are pulling you in separate directions.

115
00:09:31,800 --> 00:09:39,009
[ng]: So, it's not this kind of technical sort of mechanism that you discover.

116
00:09:39,627 --> 00:09:47,892
[ng]: It's that the eye is on the level of that pressure that you feel. It's more a direction that a...

117
00:09:48,870 --> 00:09:52,556
[ng]: Being pulled, rather than, again, some mechanism that you understand.

118
00:09:52,960 --> 00:09:59,513
[nm]: So, what you're saying is that, regardless of how particular things,

119
00:10:00,270 --> 00:10:18,153
[nm]: eye might be seeing, or how general sights the eye might be looking at, nowhere in that domain, nowhere in that field of, like, the atomic particles, or, you know, staring at the gigantic mountains, nowhere in that field is eye to be found.

120
00:10:18,593 --> 00:10:18,920
[ng]: Yeah.

121
00:10:18,920 --> 00:10:28,510
[nm]: It doesn't matter how slowly, how precisely, as I said, how meticulously, how microscopically you're looking, simply eye cannot be anywhere there.

122
00:10:29,185 --> 00:10:33,667
[ng]: Yeah, and the Buddha described it as these empty villages, or empty kind of...

123
00:10:33,667 --> 00:10:34,113
[ng]: Which one?

124
00:10:34,460 --> 00:10:35,449
[ng]: The sense bases.

125
00:10:35,501 --> 00:10:35,818
[nm]: Aha.

126
00:10:36,135 --> 00:10:38,953
[ng]: That are attacked by bandits.

127
00:10:39,500 --> 00:10:39,975
[ng]: So then...

128
00:10:39,980 --> 00:10:40,713
[nm]: Bandits being?

129
00:10:40,889 --> 00:10:42,150
[ng]: Bandits being the sense objects.

130
00:10:42,310 --> 00:10:42,667
[ng]: Aha.

131
00:10:42,720 --> 00:10:43,090
[ng]: So...

132
00:10:43,092 --> 00:10:43,606
[nm]: Raiding.

133
00:10:43,731 --> 00:10:43,931
[ng]: Yeah.

134
00:10:44,150 --> 00:10:45,018
[ng]: Being raided by bandits...

135
00:10:45,010 --> 00:10:45,569
[nm]: The empty.

136
00:10:45,870 --> 00:10:53,169
[ng]: So, if you are examining sense objects, doesn't matter how complex or special they are, you're examining the bandits.

137
00:10:53,163 --> 00:10:55,012
[ng]: You're not examining the empty village.

138
00:10:55,476 --> 00:10:55,776
[nm]: Mhmm.

139
00:10:56,166 --> 00:11:07,206
[ng]: So, that empty village can only become apparent as such precisely when you stop being concerned and engaged with the some extent of bandits that will inevitably be there.

140
00:11:07,975 --> 00:11:09,046
[nm]: Microscopic bandits.

141
00:11:09,040 --> 00:11:09,815
[ng]: Yeah...

142
00:11:10,916 --> 00:11:17,055
[nm]: So, then, the sight, sounds, smells, tastes, touches are attacking the fields.

143
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:18,618
[nm]: They're not...

144
00:11:19,006 --> 00:11:21,729
[nm]: It's not that the fields are there... Well, hold on.

145
00:11:21,726 --> 00:11:22,483
[nm]: Is that what you're saying?

146
00:11:22,483 --> 00:11:22,861
[nm]: I don't know.

147
00:11:23,003 --> 00:11:23,326
[ng]: Yeah.

148
00:11:23,400 --> 00:11:27,095
[nm]: So, it's not that the fields are there seeing sight, sounds, mouth, taste, touches.

149
00:11:27,750 --> 00:11:30,320
[nm]: Fields are there known because they're being attacked.

150
00:11:30,320 --> 00:11:30,769
[ng]: Exactly.

151
00:11:31,003 --> 00:11:31,180
[ng]: Yeah.

152
00:11:31,486 --> 00:11:32,030
[nm]: So, you can't...

153
00:11:32,033 --> 00:11:32,760
[nm]: Can you explain that?

154
00:11:32,830 --> 00:11:42,255
[ng]: So, you can't try to abstract the I as an independent thing that you find, like, first, purely by itself.

155
00:11:42,270 --> 00:11:42,590
[nm]: Why not?

156
00:11:43,295 --> 00:11:43,570
[nm]: I mean...

157
00:11:44,086 --> 00:11:45,027
[nm]: You can surely think it.

158
00:11:45,250 --> 00:11:45,584
[ng]: Yes, but...

159
00:11:46,089 --> 00:11:46,880
[nm]: What's the problem with that?

160
00:11:46,880 --> 00:11:56,966
[ng]: But the eye has to be found as an implication, when sense objects are there, but they're not being absorbed and focused on.

161
00:11:56,970 --> 00:11:58,529
[ng]: But what is this eye that I'm thinking of?

162
00:11:59,996 --> 00:12:01,606
[ng]: Well, that's another mental image.

163
00:12:02,624 --> 00:12:04,020
[nm]: And what's the problem with that?

164
00:12:05,033 --> 00:12:05,726
[ng]: Well, it's not a...

165
00:12:07,166 --> 00:12:11,329
[nm]: I can focus and not forget about it, and concentrate on it, and think about it frequently.

166
00:12:11,560 --> 00:12:12,156
[ng]: But that's the thing.

167
00:12:12,166 --> 00:12:28,366
[ng]: It's... that would... if it's divorced from the sights, if it's divorced from what's felt in regard to the sights, if it's divorced from that pressure, then that's not the actual eye that, because of which you suffer, because that you have all these assumptions in regard to.

168
00:12:28,810 --> 00:12:36,310
[ng]: So, it's the eye that has some kind of pressured relationship towards the sights.

169
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,800
[ng]: That's the one that you want to see, not just an idea of it.

170
00:12:41,073 --> 00:12:52,218
[nm]: So, if... what you're saying is, if you don't see the... or if it becomes divorced from a pressure, it ceases to be that eye, that field, that phenomenon.

171
00:12:52,227 --> 00:13:00,276
[ng]: Because it's... again, it's... if there's not that pull, then it's not that animal of the eye that the Buddha described.

172
00:13:00,520 --> 00:13:06,883
[ng]: It's just some idea that then, as much as you might develop it, is disconnected from the problem of suffering.

173
00:13:08,381 --> 00:13:24,046
[nm]: So, say, I make the effort now and catch myself when I start falling into abstractions, or focusing in concentrations on the sense objects, thinking that will reveal me the true nature of the eye, or the fields, or the village, the empty village.

174
00:13:24,855 --> 00:13:26,553
[nm]: So, how do I train rightly then?

175
00:13:27,006 --> 00:13:29,313
[nm]: Fully, to completely, fully surmount

176
00:13:30,473 --> 00:13:37,409
[nm]: That whole duality of sense objects and sense fields.

177
00:13:37,630 --> 00:13:37,753
[ng]: Right.

178
00:13:37,910 --> 00:13:40,993
[ng]: Well, it starts with the same thing that I described in the beginning.

179
00:13:40,990 --> 00:13:44,523
[ng]: You have to be... you have to have ceased...

180
00:13:44,861 --> 00:13:53,470
[ng]: specific actions towards those sense objects that automatically have you taking for granted that pressure and not seeing it by just acting out of it.

181
00:13:54,104 --> 00:14:02,498
[ng]: Then, on the basis of that, you have to keep an eye throughout your day on that pressure that will be there to some extent.

182
00:14:03,210 --> 00:14:12,923
[ng]: You have to... because as soon as you start ignoring it and overlooking it, you're automatically taking it up.

183
00:14:12,990 --> 00:14:15,363
[ng]: It's not a separate movement.

184
00:14:16,022 --> 00:14:23,087
[ng]: You're automatically failing to see that village being attacked by bandits.

185
00:14:23,086 --> 00:14:26,950
[nm]: How do I avoid falling into ignoring it and overlooking it?

186
00:14:28,220 --> 00:14:28,507
[ng]: Well, it's...

187
00:14:28,501 --> 00:14:30,680
[nm]: Do I need to concentrate on it the whole day?

188
00:14:30,913 --> 00:14:32,495
[ng]: That's the thing. It's...

189
00:14:33,538 --> 00:14:42,126
[ng]: Even if you were to go and concentrate, it wouldn't change anything about the fact of where it already is: arisen on its own.

190
00:14:43,258 --> 00:14:44,006
[nm]: So, you have to...

191
00:14:44,381 --> 00:14:47,338
[nm]: But concentrating can help me stay in touch with it, no?

192
00:14:48,270 --> 00:14:49,163
[ng]: Well, but that's the thing,

193
00:14:49,560 --> 00:14:53,110
[ng]: anything you're concentrating into is already like...

194
00:14:53,852 --> 00:14:58,504
[ng]: It's almost like you had to stay at the beginning of this path to see the crossroads, so to speak.

195
00:14:59,086 --> 00:15:03,332
[ng]: And anything that you concentrate on, you're just going down one of those crossroads.

196
00:15:04,120 --> 00:15:04,470
[ng]: So, you have...

197
00:15:04,470 --> 00:15:06,806
[nm]: Do you mean like, if I start concentrating, I lose...

198
00:15:08,061 --> 00:15:11,396
[nm]: Contact with that pressure, because of which I would be concentrating.

199
00:15:11,596 --> 00:15:20,649
[ng]: The pressure is still there, but your emphasis and your kind of weight of your attention is now directed in such a way that you won't be able to see it.

200
00:15:21,076 --> 00:15:25,692
[nm]: Okay, so how do I then not lose the sight of it without needing to concentrate on it?

201
00:15:26,410 --> 00:15:30,495
[ng]: Well, you just have to ask yourself, because again, well, first of all..

202
00:15:31,043 --> 00:15:33,566
[nm]: What if just something comes up and naturally distracts me?

203
00:15:33,700 --> 00:15:34,944
[nm]: Does that mean I lost the sight?

204
00:15:35,913 --> 00:15:42,464
[ng]: No, because even then you can be aware of how you're being pressured in regard to that new thing that arose.

205
00:15:44,353 --> 00:15:50,295
[ng]: You don't have to focus on that, you don't have to kind of overly clarify that thought.

206
00:15:51,147 --> 00:15:56,120
[ng]: You can, in most general terms, have a background awareness of how do I feel right now.

207
00:15:56,975 --> 00:16:01,655
[nm]: Yeah, but I forgot to ask that question because distracting things came up in my life.

208
00:16:02,369 --> 00:16:02,860
[ng]: Yeah, but...

209
00:16:02,864 --> 00:16:05,172
[ng]: So, does that mean I lost the touch with it?

210
00:16:05,200 --> 00:16:07,116
[nm]: Does that mean I lost the mindfulness or...?

211
00:16:08,080 --> 00:16:29,775
[ng]: If that clarity, like, that clarity might fade to some extent or the other, and that's inevitable, but what matters is, when the clarity is lost, and you kind of double down on the actions that result from the lack of perspective, then, even if you try to regain it, like, that's a difference.

212
00:16:29,775 --> 00:16:30,720
[ng]: So, if I don't,

213
00:16:31,603 --> 00:16:36,532
[nm]: Say something comes up, you know, people's lives are busy.

214
00:16:36,590 --> 00:16:45,787
[nm]: Say you had a clarity about the sense fields, and the peripheral, the pressure that's there, underneath, it and, so on.

215
00:16:46,396 --> 00:16:53,630
[nm]: Something comes up, factual requirement to deal with things that preoccupies your attention, so you lose that clarity.

216
00:16:54,510 --> 00:16:55,516
[nm]: But then you're saying now,

217
00:16:56,550 --> 00:17:01,563
[nm]: but if I don't double down in actions of, well, I presume, unsuitable kind...

218
00:17:02,473 --> 00:17:05,763
[nm]: So, say, I don't double down, but I lost the clarity because I got distracted.

219
00:17:05,763 --> 00:17:07,387
[nm]: Does that mean I lost the touch with it?

220
00:17:08,498 --> 00:17:10,584
[nm]: So, I haven't acted out, but I haven't regained...

221
00:17:10,590 --> 00:17:11,929
[nm]: I haven't maintained the clarity either.

222
00:17:12,092 --> 00:17:21,076
[ng]: Yes, but that's not a problem in itself, because you will see, if you didn't act out and double down, if you wanted to re-establish the clarity,

223
00:17:21,870 --> 00:17:23,575
[ng]: it would... it wouldn't be that difficult.

224
00:17:23,760 --> 00:17:40,716
[ng]: But if you did double down and you did just proliferate in further actions, you would see that even if you try to bring up these ideas, the whole thing is now abstract, because you're just kind of inflamed, so to speak, from all this indulgence... indulgence that you engage with.

225
00:17:40,710 --> 00:17:52,843
[ng]: So that neutral kind of forgetfulness will not... it's... it doesn't kind of undo completely...

226
00:17:53,636 --> 00:17:59,360
[ng]: rhe degree of clarity that you could have built, you could restore it just by trying to bring it back.

227
00:18:01,450 --> 00:18:02,000
[nm]: So...

228
00:18:03,560 --> 00:18:06,990
[ng]: So, that's how you could measure, am I acting out too much?

229
00:18:07,230 --> 00:18:11,572
[ng]: Am I kind of... spilling out too much.

230
00:18:12,036 --> 00:18:29,470
[ng]: It's... if afterwards you feel like all the stuff is just... before it was clear and now it feels kind of just abstract and just almost as if you hadn't clarified these things before and you have to keep restarting it from scratch every single time,

231
00:18:30,796 --> 00:18:31,443
[nm]: And you say that's...

232
00:18:31,440 --> 00:18:32,218
[ng]: ... you go back to that [?awareness].

233
00:18:32,212 --> 00:18:33,676
[nm]: because...

234
00:18:35,867 --> 00:18:38,209
[nm]: A person would have been acted out.

235
00:18:38,258 --> 00:18:38,947
[nm]: Spilling out too much.

236
00:18:38,941 --> 00:18:40,530
[nm]: Spilling out in unsuitable ways.

237
00:18:41,209 --> 00:18:54,540
[ng]: Even if they don't appear to them as unsuitable ways, if it got to the point where you can't just resume your clarity, then... and again, it feels like you have to start from scratch every time.

238
00:18:55,460 --> 00:18:56,984
[ng]: That means you were spilling out too much.

239
00:18:56,980 --> 00:19:04,338
[ng]: You were kind of allowing too much emphasis and focus on those factual things that you have to deal with, because that's the thing.

240
00:19:05,710 --> 00:19:09,286
[ng]: initially, it might feel like it's either or.

241
00:19:10,235 --> 00:19:13,155
[ng]: Like, I either maintain this clarity or I engage with these things.

242
00:19:13,750 --> 00:19:15,563
[ng]: But the more you understand it, the more you realize...

243
00:19:15,560 --> 00:19:18,332
[nm]: But as I said, sometimes you have to engage with things.

244
00:19:18,697 --> 00:19:18,897
[ng]: Yeah.

245
00:19:19,230 --> 00:19:23,249
[nm]: How do you then guarantee that you won't spill out too much?

246
00:19:24,590 --> 00:19:25,089
[nm]: Well, you have to...

247
00:19:25,080 --> 00:19:26,009
[nm]: Is there a criteria?

248
00:19:26,330 --> 00:19:28,498
[ng]: You have to be contained by the precepts, first and foremost.

249
00:19:30,424 --> 00:19:38,640
[nm]: So then, so if basically, okay, so then, spilling out too much would be, if things you have to deal with, require you to break precepts.

250
00:19:38,676 --> 00:19:38,843
[ng]: Yeah.

251
00:19:39,393 --> 00:19:41,510
[ng]: That would be the coarsest way of spilling out too much.

252
00:19:41,621 --> 00:19:46,246
[nm]: So then, doesn't matter what you need to deal with, if it's against the precepts, you don't deal with it.

253
00:19:46,250 --> 00:19:46,363
[nm]: Yeah.

254
00:19:47,061 --> 00:19:48,304
[nm]: If it's within the precepts?

255
00:19:49,130 --> 00:19:57,227
[ng]: Well, if it's within the precepts, you, like, that's what I was going to say, you, you, you got to realize that...

256
00:19:58,460 --> 00:20:09,200
[ng]: It doesn't take this kind of humongous concentrated effort to keep perspective of these things, because they're already there in your experience.

257
00:20:09,200 --> 00:20:12,387
[ng]: Like, the way you're being pressured right now, no matter what you're doing...

258
00:20:12,390 --> 00:20:13,809
[nm]: So, the pressure is already there.

259
00:20:13,852 --> 00:20:14,012
[ng]: Yeah.

260
00:20:14,295 --> 00:20:15,916
[nm]: You're contained by the precepts.

261
00:20:15,947 --> 00:20:16,120
[ng]: Yeah.

262
00:20:17,307 --> 00:20:25,944
[nm]: And then, you might, as I said, legitimately get distracted by something, but then you have those same things to come back to, because they're still there.

263
00:20:25,941 --> 00:20:26,135
[ng]: Yeah.

264
00:20:27,273 --> 00:20:34,640
[ng]: Your whole actions and your decisions that you're making, even if you were ignoring the pressure, would be based on the pressure.

265
00:20:35,101 --> 00:20:38,203
[ng]: So, it's not like it's... it only exists when you look at it.

266
00:20:38,381 --> 00:20:42,480
[nm]: So, what you're saying, you can... if you don't spill out, you can ignore it only so far.

267
00:20:42,483 --> 00:20:42,700
[nm]: Yeah.

268
00:20:43,212 --> 00:20:44,913
[nm]: Because it's there, pressuring you.

269
00:20:44,913 --> 00:20:44,929
[ng]: Yeah.
[nm]: Because it's there, pressuring you.

270
00:20:44,929 --> 00:20:45,070
[ng]: Yeah.

271
00:20:45,418 --> 00:20:55,544
[ng]: So, then, you want to tune that connection with it, I guess, you could say, so that it doesn't require you to, like, pause

272
00:20:55,904 --> 00:20:57,840
[ng]: Again, things that are not against the precepts.

273
00:20:58,289 --> 00:21:04,852
[ng]: It doesn't require you to pause completely and kind of go through these mental motions to then re-establish it.

274
00:21:04,907 --> 00:21:10,233
[ng]: You want to see, regardless of what I'm doing, you want to find, you want to be able to find it there.

275
00:21:10,810 --> 00:21:24,926
[ng]: Because if you have space to think and plan and remember and do whatever, you definitely have space for this, because all that stuff takes even more effort than being aware of what's already there.

